    decision:
      "Fresh-angle empty-backlog audit (told to favor code recent iterations had NOT re-read rather than re-auditing checkout/catalog/aria-label/SEO-meta yet again — per the last ~10 ITERATIONS entries, all five of those surfaces had just been touched). Read the commerce_sku fulfillment branch of the Stripe webhook (src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts), the CommerceOrderRecord ledger (src/lib/commerce/orders.ts), and both notify surfaces it feeds (commerceOrderPaidInquiryMessage, formatCommerceBuyNowClientReceiptHtml) end to end, specifically to check whether the adjustable-quantity Buy now feature shipped two iterations ago (commerce-checkout-adjustable-quantity, PR #613) had any downstream gap. It did: that change let a shopper buy N units of a Commerce SKU via Stripe's own quantity stepper at Checkout, and the webhook correctly ledgers session.amount_total (the real total charged, already quantity-agnostic and unaffected by the change) — but the purchased quantity itself was never captured anywhere. CommerceOrderRecord/InsertCommerceOrderInput had no quantity field, the Convex commerceOrders schema/table had no quantity column, the SQLite commerce_orders table had no quantity column, and both downstream notify surfaces (the operator inquiry-inbox message and the client/business-owner order-notification email) showed only the item name and total dollar amount with no unit count. A local business receiving 'New paid order: Latte ($15.00)' has no way to know whether that is 1 latte at $15 or 3 lattes at $5 — and critically cannot reliably back-compute it from amountPaidUsd ÷ current catalog price either, since the webhook's own commerce-sku-price-resync-metadata-match design point (an operator can re-sync a SKU's price between Session creation and completion, minting a new Stripe Price while an already-open Session still legitimately references the old one) means the catalog's *current* price is not guaranteed to be the price that was actually charged. This directly undermines client-receipt.ts's own stated purpose ('closes the Commerce order notifications sold promise') for the exact multi-unit purchases the adjustable-quantity feature was built to enable — a café selling 3 lattes in one Buy now checkout is precisely the scenario commerce-checkout-adjustable-quantity's own rationale named as the target use case, and the fulfillment ledger + both notify channels are silently blind to it.",
    rationale:
      "Added quantity as a new optional field (never assume 1 as a fallback — undefined means genuinely unknown, e.g. a pre-migration row or an unexpected line-item shape) end to end on both backends: CommerceOrderRecord/InsertCommerceOrderInput (src/lib/commerce/orders.ts); convex/schema.ts's commerceOrders table + convex/commerceOrders.ts's orderReturn validator, mapOrderRow, and the insert mutation's args/return; the SQLite commerce_orders table (new INTEGER column + ALTER TABLE migration in src/lib/db/sqlite-schema.ts, following the existing paidOutAt/refundedAt/disputedAt migration pattern) plus src/lib/commerce/sqlite-row-mapping.ts and db.ts's insertCommerceOrder INSERT statement. Deliberately preserved quantity through clearRefunded's and clearDisputed's ctx.db.replace() calls in convex/commerceOrders.ts — both rebuild the row from an explicit field list (to let optional fields clear on `replace`, since `patch(undefined)` would leave a stale value), and a field missing from that list silently vanishes the next time a refund/dispute reversal fires; this is the same field-list-completeness trap the existing paidOutAt/clientReceiptEmailSentAt/refundedAt/disputedAt entries in those two replace() calls already had to get right, so quantity had to join the same allowlist or it would have shipped working today and silently regressed on the very next reversal event. Added a new pure helper, commerceSkuLineItemQuantity (src/lib/payments/checkout-price-line-items.ts, beside its sibling sessionLineItemsIncludeMetadataKey) — reads the matched line item's own Stripe `quantity` field, returning undefined (never 0/NaN/negative) when missing or invalid, reusing the exact same listLineItems response the webhook already fetches for Price-parity verification (no second Stripe API call). Wired the resulting `quantity` local variable through to insertCommerceOrder, commerceOrderPaidInquiryMessage (catalog.ts — new optional param, only appends ', quantity N' when N>1, so the common single-unit case reads identically to before this field existed and every pre-existing test kept passing unmodified), and sendCommerceBuyNowClientReceipt/formatCommerceBuyNowClientReceiptHtml (client-receipt.ts — new optional param, renders a 'Quantity: N' line whenever quantity is known, including exactly 1, since the business-facing email is the operator's actual fulfillment instruction and consistent formatting there beats a sometimes-shown line). Also wired quantity: order.quantity into resendCommerceClientReceiptAction's (src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts) manual-resend call site, and added a small '× N' suffix (only shown when >1) to the two dashboard rows in leads-client.tsx that already render skuName/amountPaidUsd (Commerce payouts panel per-lead order list; Missing client receipt panel). New/extended Vitest coverage: checkout-price-line-items.test.ts (commerceSkuLineItemQuantity — matched/unmatched/missing/zero/negative/non-integer/blank-key cases); catalog.test.ts (quantity note appended only for >1, omitted for 1/undefined); client-receipt.test.ts (Quantity line shown when known, omitted when undefined); sqlite-row-mapping.test.ts (quantity round-trips null→undefined and a set value); convex/commerceOrders.test.ts (quantity round-trips through insert/get/list AND survives clearRefunded+clearDisputed's replace() calls; a separate case pins quantity as undefined, not 0/null, when omitted); route.test.ts (webhook passes quantity through to insertCommerceOrder/insertClientInquiry/sendCommerceBuyNowClientReceipt when the line item carries one, and passes quantity: undefined — never a fallback — when it doesn't); resend-commerce-client-receipt-action.test.ts (manual resend forwards the order's persisted quantity). Verified: fresh npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npm test (2579 passed, up from 2565 — 14 new, 190 files unchanged) + a full clean rebuild (`rm -rf .next && npm run build`, clean, same 42-route ○/ƒ split) + npm run dev sanity check (deleted local.db so the new ALTER TABLE migration runs against a fresh SQLite file, curled /, /checkout, /dashboard/leads, /preview/example-bakery, /docs/process — all 200; killed the dev server, confirmed port 3000 freed, deleted local.db afterward). All five gated NEXT_STEPS items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked and unchanged — this is a Commerce fulfillment-data-integrity gap discovered by audit (a follow-on correctness gap in a feature two iterations ago introduced, not itself resolving any gated item).",
